  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PROTECTION &amp; HYDROLOGICAL RESTORATION IN</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Marismas Nacionales Biosphere Reserve is a mosaic of river deltas, salt marshes, freshwater lagoons, estuaries, coastal lagoons, intertidal wetlands and coastal dunes. It contains the largest mangrove area on the Pacific coast and is one of the most important refueling sites for migratory birds on the Mexican Pacific Flyway. To benefit three tidal basins within the Reserve, this project will carry out a variety of activities. (1) This project will rehabilitate the hydrology of 15.53 miles of natural flood channels in mangroves, which will enhance 3,705 acres. In addition, it will enhance 494 acres and 1.86 miles of previously rehabilitated channels that were affected by Hurricane Willa in 2018. (2) This project will protect the restored sites and adjacent areas for 20 years through public or private mechanisms by: a) establishing a &apos;Sustainable Use Unit&apos;, a Conservation Agreement or an Ejido Reserve to protect 6,792.5 acres; and b) updating the &apos;Sustainable Use Unit&apos; agreement for another 494 acres. (3) This project will also determine the biological baseline of the sites; strengthen community monitoring using camera traps; implement an education campaign to reduce illicit activities among fishermen, shrimp producers, farmers and ranchers .</narrative>
  </description>
